Text: "Set theory provides a uniform language in which mathematical objects can
       be represented and compared. The primitive relation is membership,
       $x \in A$; equality is governed by extensionality; and new sets are
       obtained only through stated existence principles. The pages progress
       from the core constructors to subsets, products, Boolean operations,
       and the axiom schemes needed to justify separation, replacement,
       recursion, and comparison of cardinal size.

       The distinction between a class description and an existing set is
       essential throughout. A formula may describe all objects satisfying a
       condition without those objects forming a set. This restraint is not a
       technical nuisance: it is the mechanism that blocks the classical
       paradoxes of unrestricted comprehension."
